# Design task

Design, but do not implement, an extension of the Rust declaration CLI off main at `9e4de95` to resolve funder names. The user explicitly requires teaching the design before implementation.

The user's latest scope is authoritative: "first we need to decide what to do about the ultimate payer vs donor stuff. For now its sufficient to use companies house IDs to resolve companies without fetching the data from teh API".

Read `how.md`, `why-investigation.md`, the repository's `AGENTS.md`, and the source they cite. The historical single-name preference is evidence, not an approved policy for this feature. The current cleaner preserves donor, payer, and ultimate-payer observations separately. Company-number and kind evidence belong to donor observations only.

Produce a candidate design package in your assigned directory. Use the architect runner prompt and rationale template from `/Users/jamie/.codex/plugins/cache/skillshare-pstack-fe9f343631f3cbe0/pstack/0.9.73/skills/architect/references/`. Write proposed code only inside Markdown fences. No implementation, executable prototypes, application changes, database writes, API requests to Parliament or Companies House, or dependency installation. No further subagents.

Write caller usage first, then a module map, type/signature sketch, behavior contract, examples, and rationale. Focus on the following decisions.

- Distinguish source-role observation, company identity, and payment attribution. Define what a selected/displayed funder means, or explain why the first version should preserve separate attribution views without a single selected funder.
- Handle donor and ultimate payer both present, an explicit unnamed/withheld ultimate payer, `is_ultimate_payer_different` true/false/absent, two differently named roles, donor/payer disagreements, declaration-level names with nested payments, and parent references. Do not silently equate unknown with same.
- The legacy `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` uses `IsUltimatePayerDifferent` to suppress parent fallback. Do not invent the stronger claim that `false` proves donor and ultimate-payer observations identify the same entity. Inspect source context and state unresolved field semantics explicitly.
- Resolve company observations using already captured company identifiers, without enrichment. State how IDs are parsed and scoped without inventing registry validation or padding missing digits. Keep the difference between a source-reported company number and independently verified registry membership clear.
- Equal company identifiers can establish a source-reported shared company identity despite different names. Matching names alone must not transfer donor metadata to a payer or ultimate payer. Different company IDs must not merge through name similarity.
- Describe missing IDs, malformed IDs, withheld/missing names, personal names, conjunctions, conflicting kinds, and conflicting role flags. Separate row outcomes from a corrupt dataset or I/O failure.
- Propose the minimum useful offline output, deterministic identity keys, a safe repeat/publication policy, and a service/storage contract matching the Rust codebase. Do not add DB schema or use the website text-search API. A future database interaction must use SQLx macros.
- Explain how downstream consumers count each funding occurrence once without accidentally double-counting role links. No fabricated money rows for declaration-only observations.
- Include a compact acceptance-case table. Clearly mark the attribution decision as a proposal for the user to discuss before implementation.

The three assigned design directions must be structurally distinct. Candidate 1 should develop an observation-to-identity mapping with attribution deferred to explicit views. Candidate 2 should develop a typed payment-attribution decision model alongside identity mapping. Candidate 3 should develop a relationship model connecting payments, role observations, and identities. All must solve the same scoped user task and compare against at least one other whole design.

Write `design.md` and nothing outside your assigned candidate directory. Keep it reviewable, roughly 100 to 180 lines. Read relevant principle leaf skills before citing them. Include explicit design-red-flag screening.
# Design rubric

Score each dimension from 1 to 5. The first two are correctness requirements, not optional tradeoffs.

1. Attribution semantics preserve donor/payer/ultimate-payer distinctions, withheld/missing evidence, explicit flags, source scopes and payment multiplicity.
2. Identity uses captured company identifiers without API enrichment, false name-based merges, or cross-role metadata propagation.
3. Interface depth gives CLI callers one coherent service operation and puts policy in typed domain models with storage-only adapters.
4. Scope is useful and small for the user's request. No generic graph framework, manual-review platform, or database migration without need.
5. Deterministic outputs, explainable outcomes, repeat behavior and boundary validation have concrete acceptance cases.

Check every candidate against architect/references/design-red-flags.md. Select a base before grafting. The output is a proposal to teach, not authorization to implement.
# Captured role semantics

Read-only inspection on 7 October 2026 used DuckDB with an in-memory database. No data file or application file changed. Input was the existing raw capture at `/Users/jamie/development/exposed/exposed/data/01a10cb4-4148-7513-bcb1-da318ca2ba93/raw/declarations/*.parquet`.

The retained source field description directly resolves the flag semantics:

> Whether the ultimate payer is different to the payer specified in linked parent interest

`UltimatePayerName` is described as:

> Name of the ultimate payer (if different to parent interest)

These descriptions occur in latest source versions for declarations 5900 and 13091. Thus the flag compares the child to its linked parent payer. It is not evidence that donor and ultimate-payer observations in the same occurrence are identical.

Declaration 13091 points to parent 11317, has flag `true`, and names `Viking Penguin` as ultimate payer. Parent 11317's latest source names `Viking- Penguin via Blake Friedmann Literary, TV & Film Agency Ltd` as payer. Its raw projection has null funding names because it has no funding anchor. The current cleaner recovers this declaration-scoped payer from retained source JSON.

Declaration 5900 points to parent 5222, has flag `false`, and has no local donor, payer, or ultimate-payer name. Parent attribution is therefore a concrete requirement for this shape.

The raw capture contains 11,169 projected rows, including placeholder rows. It has 310 rows with an ultimate-payer name; all 310 also have flag true and a parent, and none also has a donor name. Filtering to rows with amount or payment type gives 1,498 rows with flag false and a parent and no local name, 310 with flag true and a parent, and 6,891 with a null flag and no parent. These are counts from one capture, not whole-project coverage or a performance measurement. They do not establish that other combinations are impossible.

The source-description query was:

```sql
SELECT DISTINCT r.declaration_id, f.value
FROM read_parquet('/Users/jamie/development/exposed/exposed/data/01a10cb4-4148-7513-bcb1-da318ca2ba93/raw/declarations/*.parquet') r,
     json_each(r.source_json, '$.versions') v,
     json_each(v.value, '$.fields') f
WHERE r.declaration_id IN (13091, 11317, 5900)
  AND json_extract(v.value, '$.register.id')::BIGINT = r.register_id
  AND json_extract_string(f.value, '$.name') IN
      ('PayerName', 'UltimatePayerName', 'IsUltimatePayerDifferent',
       'DonorName', 'DonorCompanyIdentifier');
```

The count query was:

```sql
SELECT is_ultimate_payer_different,
       count(*) AS rows,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE parent_declaration_id IS NOT NULL) AS with_parent,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE donor_name IS NULL AND payer_name IS NULL
                        AND ultimate_payer_name IS NULL) AS no_local_name
FROM read_parquet('/Users/jamie/development/exposed/exposed/data/01a10cb4-4148-7513-bcb1-da318ca2ba93/raw/declarations/*.parquet')
WHERE amount IS NOT NULL OR payment_type IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY 1;
```

Design consequence: evaluate an explicit same-member parent-payer reference for flag false. Flag true blocks parent attribution, even if the explicit ultimate payer is absent or withheld. An absent flag is unknown, not evidence for parent inheritance. Do not parse the `via` string into identities.

Parent 5222's latest source payer is `Guardian News & Media Ltd`. This gives a complete example for child 5900 without fetching Parliament again.

## Captured company-number quality

The same capture has 2,467 raw rows with a non-null company number, all with source kind `Company`. Of these, 2,197 match eight digits or two letters followed by six digits after trimming. This pattern was an exploratory count, not a complete Companies House validator. Other values include `527227`, `0967733649`, `123`, and `NI 016363`.

Two primary documentation sources were read. No company-record API was called.

- [Companies House number-entry guidance](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company-lookup/search?forward=/auth-code-requests/company/%7BcompanyNumber%7D/confirm) specifies an eight-character number and instructs users to add leading zeros to shorter values. It gives a numeric example. A proposed numeric padding rule can cite this guidance; it is not proof that the source supplied the correct number.
- [Companies House URI guide](https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/426891/uniformResourceIdentifiersCustomerGuide.pdf), version 1.1, says prefixes use uppercase and lists legacy forms including `R0`. Its old prefix list is not a current exhaustive validator.

Design consequence: distinguish mechanical canonicalization from registry verification. Do not implement a guessed exhaustive prefix validator. Leave unusual or ambiguous numbers unresolved in the first version and retain their raw spelling. Deciding whether to pad short numeric values is an explicit policy choice supported by the entry guidance, not an API lookup.
# Resolve observations; select a role only when reporting

## Usage, caller's view

Proposal for discussion before implementation: keep donor, payer and ultimate-payer reporting as explicit, separate views. Do not publish a single selected funder. A declared-role view answers who was named in that role; an ultimate-source view also follows an explicitly supported parent-payer reference. A missing ultimate payer stays unknown unless that parent rule supplies evidence, even when the donor has a company number.

The proposed `declarations resolve --key <ingestion-key>` stage reads the existing cleaned pair and publishes an observation-to-company mapping offline. It neither fetches registry records nor rewrites funding amounts. Exact command spelling follows the existing CLI when implemented.

```rust
// CLI: the filesystem adapter already owns the explicit ingestion key.
let summary = DeclarationResolverService::new(storage)
    .resolve_declarations().await?;
println!("{} observations linked", summary.company_links());

// Consumer: explicitly ask the donor question, one row per occurrence.
let resolved = ResolvedDeclarations::new(cleaned, mapping)?;
for occurrence in resolved.funding_view(FundingView::DeclaredRole(FunderRole::Donor)) {
    display(occurrence.entry(), occurrence.observation(), occurrence.resolution());
}

// Consumer: ultimate-payer coverage includes absent and withheld observations.
let ultimate_rows = resolved.funding_view(FundingView::UltimateSource);
let coverage = count_available_names(ultimate_rows); // Not an identity count.
```

## Problem

The current `funder_id` names a source-role observation. Equal names do not establish identity, and donor company evidence does not belong to an ultimate payer. The legacy preferred-name rule mixes those questions. The next useful step is narrower: join observations carrying compatible source-reported company identifiers while leaving attribution visible to callers. This uses the cleaner's role references and stable occurrence IDs without changing the database's current unique-name model.

## Shape and module ownership

| Module | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `domain/models/declaration_resolution.rs` | Company-number parsing policy, observation outcomes, deterministic mapping, checked aggregate and explicit role views. Private collection fields prevent bypassing link invariants; parent-payer selection belongs to the derived view, not identity mapping. |
| `domain/repositories/declaration_resolution.rs` | Read a coherent typed cleaned dataset; publish a complete resolution artifact. No Arrow types escape. |
| `domain/services/declaration_resolution.rs` | One read/resolve/publish operation and summary; matches `DeclarationCleanerService`. |
| `outbound/declaration_resolution.rs` | Cleaned Parquet decoding, manifest digests, mapping encoding and atomic publication. Reuse filesystem paths and existing error conventions. |
| `inbound/cli.rs` and ingestion-stage enum | Add offline `resolve`; require key and loader configuration only. |

The service hides integrity checks, deterministic grouping and publication behind one call. The consumer selects its reporting question. Roles remain the existing `FunderRole` enum; there is no second list of role names. The pure model owns resolution, while the adapter parses wire fields and rejects structural corruption, per `boundary-discipline`.

```rust
// Signature sketch; bodies intentionally omitted. Existing ID and role types are reused.
pub struct ReportedCompanyNumber(String); // Private canonical eight-character token.
pub enum CompanyNumberEvidence {
    Absent,
    Accepted(ReportedCompanyNumber),
    UnsupportedFormat { raw: String },
}
impl CompanyNumberEvidence {
    pub fn parse(raw: Option<&str>) -> Self;
}
pub struct CompanyIdentityKey(ReportedCompanyNumber);
pub enum UnresolvedReason {
    NoCompanyNumber,
    UnsupportedCompanyNumber,
    ConflictingDonorKind,
    UnsupportedDonorKind,
}
pub enum ObservationResolution {
    SourceReportedCompany { key: CompanyIdentityKey },
    Unresolved { reason: UnresolvedReason },
}
pub struct ObservationMapping {
    observation: FunderObservationId,
    resolution: ObservationResolution,
}
pub struct ResolutionMapping { rows: Vec<ObservationMapping> }
impl ResolutionMapping {
    pub fn resolve(cleaned: &CleanedDeclarations) -> Self;
}
pub struct ResolvedDeclarations {
    cleaned: CleanedDeclarations,
    mapping: ResolutionMapping,
    // Private indexes by observation ID and (member ID, parent declaration ID); root scope parsed once.
}
pub enum FundingView { DeclaredRole(FunderRole), UltimateSource }
pub enum AttributionOrigin { LocalRole, ParentPayer }
pub enum MissingAttribution { NoEvidence, MissingParentPayer, AmbiguousParentPayer, ConflictingEvidence }
pub enum ViewAttribution<'a> {
    Observed { observation: &'a FunderObservation, origin: AttributionOrigin },
    Missing(MissingAttribution),
}
pub struct FundingRoleView<'a> {
    // Private references to exactly one entry, zero/one observation and its outcome.
    entry: &'a CleanedFundingEntry,
    attribution: ViewAttribution<'a>,
    resolution: Option<&'a ObservationResolution>,
}
impl ResolvedDeclarations {
    pub fn new(cleaned: CleanedDeclarations, mapping: ResolutionMapping)
        -> Result<Self, EntityIngestionError>;
    pub fn funding_view(&self, view: FundingView)
        -> impl Iterator<Item = FundingRoleView<'_>>;
}
// FundingRoleView has entry(), observation(), resolution(), attribution() accessors.
pub trait DeclarationResolutionStorage: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn read_cleaned_declarations(&self)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<CleanedDeclarations, EntitySearchPipelineError>> + Send;
    fn publish_resolution(&self, result: &ResolutionMapping)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<(), EntitySearchPipelineError>> + Send;
}
pub struct DeclarationResolverService<S> { storage: S }
impl<S: DeclarationResolutionStorage> DeclarationResolverService<S> {
    pub const fn new(storage: S) -> Self;
    pub async fn resolve_declarations(&self)
        -> Result<DeclarationResolutionSummary, EntityIngestionError>;
}
```

## Behavior contract and attribution policy

| Evidence | Proposed interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| Donor and ultimate payer both named | Preserve both. Each role view selects only its own observation, even if names match. The ultimate-source view uses the explicit ultimate observation. |
| Ultimate payer explicitly empty or withheld | Ultimate-payer view reports missing or withheld; never falls back to donor. |
| Ultimate-payer flag `true` | Preserve the claim of difference; absent ultimate-payer name remains unknown. No parent fallback. |
| Flag `false` | Ultimate-source view may use the same-member parent root payer; never equate local donor and ultimate payer. |
| Flag absent | Unknown flag, distinct from `false`; no implied agreement. |
| Donor and payer names disagree | Both remain usable role observations. Neither overrides the other. |
| Top-level names and nested occurrences | Declaration observations resolve independently; never propagate them to nested funding entries. |
| Parent reference | Resolve only a direct, unique, same-member root-level parent payer for the ultimate-source view and flag false. No amount or metadata copying. |

Captured descriptions now settle the comparison: `IsUltimatePayerDifferent` compares the child ultimate payer with the payer in the linked parent interest. It never compares the child's donor and ultimate payer. `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` also allowed absent flags, but this proposal permits inheritance only for explicit false. The captured case 5900 has no local names and flag false; ignoring parent attribution would leave all 1,498 financial rows of that shape unresolved, including this demonstrated parent-payer link. See `../source-evidence.md`.

`UltimateSource` first retains any explicit local ultimate-payer observation, including missing/withheld. Otherwise false plus a parent resolves a unique root-level payer observation indexed by member ID and parent declaration ID. Retain its observation ID, role and scope, and mark attribution `ParentPayer`; do not clone it as a child ultimate observation. An explicit withheld/empty local ultimate observation blocks inheritance. True blocks inheritance; absent stays unknown. Local donor or payer observations never substitute for the parent payer. If explicit false accompanies a local ultimate name inconsistent with the available parent name, emit `ConflictingEvidence` while both observations remain accessible in declared-role views. Equal names do not merge identities.

Missing parent evidence yields `MissingParentPayer`; multiple candidate parent payer observations yield `AmbiguousParentPayer`. Cross-member matches are ignored; self-parent references produce conflicting evidence. There is no recursive traversal, so a cycle cannot loop or supply a multi-hop fallback. A root payer may be declaration-scoped or funding-scoped when the parent has its own top-level amount. At decoding, classify its source pointer as exactly `/versions/<index>/fields`; never select `/values/` groups by ordinal or name. Only this direct parent root payer is supported; nested parent funding roles and multi-hop ancestry supply no fallback. Parent declaration-only observations need no amount or inherited flag. Child 5900 therefore refers to parent 5222's `Guardian News & Media Ltd` payer without creating a parent money row. Source replay would be needed before adding broader ancestry rules. The case 13091 uses explicit `Viking Penguin` with true; its parent 11317 payer stays distinct and its `via` text is never split.

Company parsing trims outer whitespace and uppercases ASCII letters; it preserves every digit, including leading zeros. Version 1 accepts exactly eight ASCII alphanumeric characters containing at least one digit as a conservative token envelope. This is an explicit supported-format policy, not a catalogue of valid Companies House prefixes or proof of registry membership. Other nonblank forms produce `UnsupportedCompanyNumber`; never pad, delete punctuation, infer a country, or call an API. Empty input is absent. Retain the original value through the observation reference.

The identity namespace is `source-reported:companies-house:<canonical-token>`. Its claim means that Parliament supplied this company identifier, not that the registry independently confirmed it. Missing donor kind or explicit `Company` permits that link. Explicit incompatible kinds produce `ConflictingDonorKind`; unknown kind text produces `UnsupportedDonorKind`. A conflicted observation stays unresolved even if another observation with the same number resolves. No last-write-wins entity kind exists.

Two eligible observations with the same identifier share a key despite different names. Different identifiers always remain different keys. Names, aliases, initials and token similarity do not merge any identities. Withheld names and missing names can still resolve from eligible identifier evidence. Personal-looking names or conjunctions are not split or classified from spelling; a compatible captured number still makes only a source-reported company claim. Otherwise each observation remains unresolved, with its original name status available to reports.

Current payer and ultimate-payer observations cannot receive a company link because cleaning attaches company numbers only to donors. Even an exact donor-name match does not change that. This limited coverage is honest and can expand later through a separately justified identity-evidence rule.

## Offline artifacts, consistency and counting

Publish `resolved/declarations/observation_resolution.parquet` plus a manifest. The table has exactly one row per observation: `observation_id`, `outcome`, nullable `company_identity_key`, nullable `unresolved_reason`. Outcome-specific column constraints come from the domain enum. The manifest records schema/resolver versions, input-file SHA-256 digests and row counts. No separate entity table or preferred-name record is necessary. For display, use the linked observation's original name, or the reported number if unnamed.

Sort mapping rows by observation ID. Identity keys are independent of ingestion key and row order; unresolved observations get no invented shared entity key. Observation IDs remain scoped to the manifest's exact dataset, since ordinal changes between captures can change their meaning. Equal keys across runs express equal source-reported identifiers, not a claim that observations are unchanged.

The adapter reads each cleaned file once into the checked dataset and binds its publication to those input digests. Reject missing columns, invalid enums, duplicate observation/occurrence IDs, wrong role/scope references, inconsistent ordinals and dangling funding references before writing. Malformed company strings, withheld names and conflicting donor kinds are row outcomes and do not fail the run. Missing files and read/write failures use storage errors; structural input failures use the existing data/read-error conventions with path context.

Write both artifacts into a unique sibling staging directory, close and sync them, then publish with an atomic no-replace directory operation. A completed existing destination is an explicit error; a repeat never silently overwrites. Concurrent attempts own different staging directories and only one can publish, per `separate-before-serializing-shared-state`. A crash leaves either a complete result or an ignored staging directory. A future policy version requires a new run/output location, not editing an existing artifact.

Each view iterates funding entries first and selects at most one observation, locally or through the direct parent index. It emits one row for every occurrence, including absent roles, so an amount appears once per view. A report must choose one versioned view policy for financial totals; unioning roles is a relationship report, not an additive money table. To ask whether a company occurs in any role, select distinct funding-entry IDs before joining amounts. Declaration-scoped observations never create funding rows. Currency/amount parsing remains outside this identity stage.

## Acceptance cases

| Input | Required result |
| --- | --- |
| `Acme Ltd / 01234567` and `Acme Holdings / 01234567` on eligible donors | One company key, two observations; all occurrences retained. |
| Same donor name with `01234567` and `07654321` | Two company keys; no name bridge. |
| Donor has number; identical ultimate-payer name | Donor resolves; ultimate payer remains unresolved. |
| Donor named; ultimate payer withheld; flag true | Ultimate view keeps withheld observation, with no fallback. |
| Flag false; no local ultimate name; unique same-member parent payer | Ultimate-source view references parent payer with provenance; donor remains separate. |
| Flag absent, or true with unnamed ultimate payer | No parent fallback; ultimate source remains unknown. |
| Flag false with local ultimate name disagreeing with parent payer | Conflicting evidence in ultimate-source view; declared-role views preserve names. |
| `1234567`, `SC-12345`, whitespace-only identifier | Unsupported, unsupported, absent respectively; no fabricated digits. |
| ` sc123456 ` and `SC123456`, eligible kinds | Same source-reported key; no registry-verification claim. |
| Number with `Individual`; same number elsewhere with `Company` | Individual observation unresolved; compatible company observation resolves. |
| Missing/withheld name with eligible number | Company link with name status unchanged. |
| `Jane Doe`, `A and B`, no number | Separate unresolved observations; no person merge or splitting. |
| Top-level payer, two nested entries lacking payer, no supported parent rule | Declaration observation retained; two missing payer-view rows, no local propagation. |
| Parent missing, wrong member, multiple parent payer observations, self-reference | Missing or ambiguous/conflicting attribution outcome; never a guessed payer. |
| Duplicate identical payments | Distinct occurrence IDs and two view rows; no deduplication by name/value. |
| Malformed reference, I/O failure, existing final destination | Fail with no new completed output. |

## Tradeoffs, alternatives and review

We accept unresolved payer identities and explicit report-role selection in exchange for a small, auditable identity rule. We accept conservative unsupported-number outcomes rather than guessing corrections. The most consequential product decision remains this proposal to offer separate role views without a preferred funder.

A typed payment-attribution decision model would hide fallback and withholding rules behind a single answer per occurrence. It is better once the user chooses a preferred funding question, but today it would encode unsettled role semantics into every result. A general relationship graph would express arbitrary ancestry and controller relationships, but callers would need traversal and cardinality rules for ordinary totals. The mapping plus fixed role views hides those joins without introducing that graph language.

Red-flag screening: no public Arrow schemas; no duplicated role registry; one pure owner of company eligibility and identity keys; one publication writer; no intermediate persistence stages; no generic matching engine or pass-through search service. The service's storage boundary is justified by offline I/O and atomic publication, not merely forwarding a model call. A consumer cannot construct an unchecked aggregate or modify its private indexes. Parent attribution is derived from checked evidence in this module rather than copied into the identity mapping.

Synthesis decision: reserved for the parent architect. Implementation reconciliation: none; this package contains no implementation.

Open question for the checkpoint: does the user want these explicit role views, or a single preferred-funder report with a separately approved fallback policy? Is the conservative eight-character envelope sufficient initially, retaining known short numeric values, `NI 016363` and ten-digit strings as unsupported? Source entry guidance supports numeric padding, but this candidate deliberately defers that canonicalization choice.

After the user has been taught and accepted the design, first implement the checked cleaned-input reader and pure mapping against the acceptance cases. No database schema change or website search dependency is needed; any later database adapter must use SQLx macros.
# Candidate 2: payment decisions with separate identity evidence

## Usage, caller's view

Proposal for discussion before implementation: each funding occurrence receives one typed reporting decision. Prefer an explicitly named ultimate payer; use the linked parent payer when the child explicitly reports the same ultimate payer; otherwise use donor or payer as a clearly labelled fallback only when evidence permits. A fallback means "reported donor" or "reported payer", never "confirmed ultimate funder".

The proposed offline command is `exposed declarations resolve --ingestion-key <key>`. It reads the existing cleaned pair and publishes a new resolved directory. It requires neither `DATABASE_URL` nor an API client. The actual command spelling should follow the existing CLI stage parser.

```rust
// CLI owns configuration and invokes one service operation.
let summary = DeclarationResolutionService::new(storage)
    .resolve(key, AttributionPolicy::UltimateAwareV1).await?;

// Report consumer handles a decision without guessing its role or confidence.
match occurrence.attribution() {
    AttributionDecision::Selected(selection) => report(selection.subject(), selection.basis()),
    AttributionDecision::Unattributed(reason) => report_unknown(reason),
}

// Identity browsing retains observations even when no payment is attributable.
for observation in resolved.observations_for(&company_key) {
    display_source_role(observation.role(), observation.source_pointer());
}
```

## Problem and module map

Cleaning preserves donor, payer and ultimate-payer observations; only donor observations carry company numbers. `FundingEntryId` identifies money occurrences, while `FunderObservationId` identifies evidence. Choosing a reporting role cannot resolve identity, and resolving a company cannot prove that it ultimately paid.

| Owner | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `domain/models/declaration_resolution.rs` | Private constructors, identity evidence, attribution outcomes, pure whole-bundle resolution |
| `domain/services/declaration_resolution.rs` | Load typed cleaned input, resolve, publish, return summary |
| `domain/ports/declaration_resolution_storage.rs` | Read coherent inputs and publish one coherent result |
| `outbound/declaration_resolution.rs` | Arrow/Parquet parsing, referential checks, manifest and filesystem publication |
| Existing ingestion CLI | Add resolve stage and explicit key, construct only filesystem dependencies |

One model owns the decision policy. Callers do not chain role selection, metadata copying and matching helpers. The service hides those operations behind one command while exposing their recorded results. Wire columns remain inside the adapter. No DB schema or website search changes are required; a later database adapter must use SQLx macros.

## Shape

```rust
// Proposed sketches only. Fields are private; checked bundle construction is adapter-owned.
struct CompanyNumber(String);
enum CompanyNumberEvidence {
    Missing,
    Parsed { number: CompanyNumber, source: FunderObservationId },
    Rejected { raw: String, reason: CompanyNumberProblem },
}
enum IdentityKey {
    SourceReportedCompany(CompanyNumber), // rendered as companies-house/reported/<number>
    UnresolvedObservation(FunderObservationId), // run-scoped, not a person identity
}
struct ObservationResolution {
    observation: FunderObservationId,
    identity: IdentityKey,
    company_evidence: CompanyNumberEvidence,
    issues: Vec<ObservationIssue>,
}
enum UltimateDifference { ReportedDifferent, ReportedNotDifferent, Unspecified }
enum SelectionBasis { ExplicitUltimatePayer, LinkedParentPayer, DonorFallback, PayerFallback }
enum SelectedAttribution {
    Local { observation: FunderObservationId, basis: LocalSelectionBasis },
    LinkedParent { parent: DeclarationId, payer: FunderObservationId },
} // subject identity and SelectionBasis are derived, never synchronized copies
enum LocalSelectionBasis { ExplicitUltimatePayer, DonorFallback, PayerFallback }
enum AttributionDecision {
    Selected(SelectedAttribution),
    Unattributed(UnattributedReason),
}
enum UnattributedReason {
    UltimateWithheld, UltimateUnnamed, DifferentUltimateNotIdentified,
    NoLocalSubject, ContextRequiresInterpretation, ParentPayerUnavailable, ParentRoleConflict,
}
struct OccurrenceResolution {
    funding: FundingEntryId,
    decision: AttributionDecision,
    issues: Vec<AttributionIssue>,
}
struct ResolvedDeclarations {
    observations: BTreeMap<FunderObservationId, ObservationResolution>,
    occurrences: BTreeMap<FundingEntryId, OccurrenceResolution>,
    // Company-to-observation index is derived from observations for in-memory browsing.
}
enum AttributionPolicy { UltimateAwareV1 }
impl ResolvedDeclarations {
    fn resolve(input: CheckedCleanedDeclarations, policy: AttributionPolicy) -> Self;
    fn observations_for(&self, company: &IdentityKey) -> impl Iterator<Item = &ResolvedObservation>;
}
impl CompanyNumber {
    fn parse_source(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, CompanyNumberProblem>;
}
trait DeclarationResolutionStorage {
    async fn read_cleaned(&self, key: &IngestionKey)
        -> Result<CheckedCleanedDeclarations, DeclarationResolutionError>;
    async fn publish(&self, key: &IngestionKey, result: &ResolvedDeclarations)
        -> Result<PublicationReceipt, DeclarationResolutionError>;
}
impl<S: DeclarationResolutionStorage> DeclarationResolutionService<S> {
    fn new(storage: S) -> Self;
    async fn resolve(&self, key: IngestionKey, policy: AttributionPolicy)
        -> Result<ResolutionSummary, DeclarationResolutionError>;
}
```

`CheckedCleanedDeclarations`, `ResolvedObservation`, issues and summary are domain types. `ResolvedObservation` is a borrowed view combining retained source evidence and its resolution. `SelectedAttribution::subject()` is likewise a borrowed view returned through its owning occurrence. Source roles and name statuses reuse existing enums. Parsing and cross-reference checks happen once at the input boundary; pure resolution returns row outcomes, not validation errors. Private constructors permit only local occurrence roles or an eligible same-member linked-parent declaration payer. A parent selection records the referenced declaration and observation IDs.

## Behavior contract and policy

The table is ordered. "UP present" means a source ultimate-payer observation exists, including an empty name. A usable subject has a present name or an accepted company identifier, though current UP/payer inputs have no company identifiers. Issues preserve every source flag and disagreement.

| Source evidence | Reporting decision under proposed `UltimateAwareV1` |
| --- | --- |
| UP has present usable name | Select UP; retain donor and payer independently |
| UP is withheld | Unattributed, even if donor or payer is usable |
| UP is explicitly empty | Unattributed with `UltimateUnnamed` |
| No UP and flag is true | Unattributed with `DifferentUltimateNotIdentified` |
| No UP, flag false, unique usable linked-parent declaration payer | Select parent payer as `LinkedParentPayer` |
| No UP, flag false, parent/payer absent, withheld or ambiguous | Unattributed with specific parent reason; no intermediary fallback |
| No UP, flag absent, usable local donor | Select donor as `DonorFallback` |
| No UP, flag absent, no usable donor, usable local payer | Select payer as `PayerFallback` |
| No usable local subject and declaration/parent context exists | Unattributed with `ContextRequiresInterpretation` |
| No usable local subject or context | Unattributed with `NoLocalSubject` |

False does not link donor and ultimate-payer identities. The captured descriptions in `source-evidence.md` directly say the flag compares the child ultimate payer to the payer in its linked parent interest. False therefore supports parent attribution; absent supplies no such support. The legacy `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` suppresses fallback for true but also permits absent. This proposal deliberately requires explicit false for parent selection. True blocks parent attribution even with absent or withheld UP.

An explicit local UP remains first. False plus a different local UP/parent-payer spelling records a parent-role disagreement without asserting distinct identities from names. Donor/UP name differences alone are not flag conflicts. True plus independently supported equal child/parent company identity records conflicting role evidence and retains explicit UP. A present empty/withheld UP blocks fallback, including parent fallback, and records conflict if false names a usable parent.

Parent selection follows exactly one `parent_declaration_id` in the same member and captured latest-version dataset, and requires a unique declaration-scoped payer observation with usable name. Missing parent, unusable payer or competing parent versions produce row-level parent outcomes. No recursive inheritance, funding-role inference, `via` splitting or guessed cross-member reference is allowed. Actual self/cyclic references produce a contextual issue and no parent selection. Declaration-level names in the current child alongside nested payments remain contextual; the supported parent rule does not license arbitrary top-level inheritance. Donor/payer disagreements retain both observations; donor wins only the documented fallback decision. Malformed scope IDs or cross-member observation links are dataset errors.

Company identity uses the captured donor number, never a name lookup. Proposed narrow local parsing trims outer ASCII whitespace, uppercases ASCII letters and accepts exactly eight ASCII alphanumeric characters with at least one digit. It preserves leading zeros and rejects internal punctuation, short values and unsupported forms without padding. This is an explicit input acceptance grammar to discuss, not a catalogue of Companies House registry formats or evidence that a registry record exists. Unsupported genuine formats remain unresolved rather than repaired.

The namespace is Companies House source-reported identifiers from this captured field. Accepted equal identifiers produce the same company key across runs despite differing names; differing identifiers always remain separate. A missing kind or `Company` kind permits an identifier anchor. An explicit non-company or unknown kind plus a number yields an unresolved observation and a kind-conflict issue. Names, personal-looking names and conjunctions never split or join identity; a conjunction with a number records a warning that the whole observation reports that identifier. Missing or withheld donor names can still resolve a reported company number without inventing a public name. All rejected raw values survive in evidence.

Current payer/UP observations have no number, so they remain unresolved even when their names equal a resolved donor's name. A future independently captured same-number observation could share the company key, retaining the donor as the original donor-metadata source. No metadata is copied across roles. Unresolved keys include the ingestion key when serialized and do not assert equality across runs.

## Output, counting and publication

Publish `observation_resolution.parquet`, keyed uniquely by observation ID; `payment_attribution.parquet`, keyed uniquely by funding-entry ID; and one manifest containing schema version, policy version, input content digest, output row counts and deterministic bundle digest. Observation rows record identity outcome, parsed/raw company evidence and issue codes. Attribution rows record selected observation, basis, parent reference when applicable, or reason and issue codes. Retained cleaned artifacts supply names, scope, roles, flags, amounts and source pointers; the manifest binds results to those exact inputs.

Count money from cleaned funding entries joined one-to-one to payment attribution. Then look up the selected observation's identity. Never sum money after joining every role observation. Unknown decisions remain a distinct reporting bucket. Declaration-only observations appear in identity output and produce zero attribution rows. Repeated equal payments keep distinct occurrence IDs.

Sort output by stable IDs, canonicalize issue ordering and exclude execution timestamps from content digests. Publication writes a unique staging sibling, closes writers and atomically renames to `resolved/declarations`. An existing exact matching manifest and verified file digests returns `AlreadyPublished`; an existing conflicting or damaged bundle fails without replacement. Concurrent processes each stage independently; the loser verifies the winning result before reporting success. Crashes expose no partial destination and leave only private staging. A deliberate changed policy uses a new versioned destination rather than overwriting history.

Corrupt schema, duplicate IDs, missing role references, mismatched role/scope or input fingerprints abort before publication. Missing names, malformed numbers, ambiguous roles and unsupported kinds are inspectable row outcomes. Read/write failures use domain resolution errors with filesystem context; they do not masquerade as unresolved funders.

## Acceptance examples

| Case | Required result |
| --- | --- |
| Donor A, UP B, donor number `01234567` | UP B selected unresolved; donor A maps to reported company; no number transfer |
| Donor A, UP withheld, false or absent | Unknown ultimate payer; donor retained and not selected |
| Donor A, absent UP, true | Unknown different ultimate payer; no fallback |
| Donor A, absent UP, false versus absent | False selects eligible parent payer or remains unknown; absent permits donor fallback; no donor/UP identity implication |
| Donor A and payer B, absent UP/flag | Donor fallback; payer B retained separately |
| Two donors, names A/B, same accepted number | One reported company key, two source observations and occurrences |
| Equal names, distinct accepted company IDs | Distinct company keys; no fuzzy merge |
| Donor name withheld, accepted ID, absent UP/flag | Donor fallback with company identity and withheld display name |
| Short number, person name, conjunction or non-company kind | Preserve raw evidence and relevant issue; no name-based merge or split |
| Same-member parent payer plus unnamed child payment, false | Select parent payer once; keep child money row and parent observation |
| Parent payer plus child UP Viking Penguin, true | Select child UP; preserve parent `via` name unsplit |
| Parent payer plus child flag absent or true without UP | Absent does not inherit; true remains unknown |
| Missing/withheld/cyclic parent with false | Row-level unattributed parent reason; no donor substitution |
| Child top-level payer plus unnamed nested payment | Context unresolved; no arbitrary top-level inheritance |
| Declaration-only donor with accepted ID | Identity row, zero funding or attribution rows |
| Second identical run or crash during staging | Verified existing bundle succeeds; no replacement or partial published bundle |

## Rationale and alternatives

We accept conservative unknown identity for payer/UP in exchange for company resolution supported by captured evidence. We accept a donor fallback that does not prove ultimate funding in exchange for a useful one-row reporting result whose basis is explicit. Consumers must label fallback totals accordingly.

An identity-only mapping with explicit role views has a smaller command contract and leaves attribution fully open. It loses here because each reporting consumer must repeat withholding, flag and fallback rules. A relationship graph hides traversal and evidence storage well, but callers must still choose payment attribution and avoid multiplying money through role edges; it adds a general traversal interface beyond this command's needs. This typed decision model centralizes that reporting policy while retaining observations for later reinterpretation.

Red-flag screening: one public service operation; private Arrow types; one policy owner; one publication owner; no exposed internal stage sequence; no no-op legacy persistence path; identity derived from observation evidence and selected identity derived by lookup rather than synchronized copies. The in-memory reverse index is derived, never published as another writable truth.

Synthesis decision belongs to the parent review; this candidate does not select itself. Implementation reconciliation is empty because no implementation is authorized at this checkpoint.

Open questions for discussion: should primary reporting prefer explicit ultimate payer with labelled donor fallback, or should donor and ultimate-payer reports remain separately selected views? Is the narrow company-number grammar appropriate for the captured data? Should conflicting explicit UP and false parent evidence retain explicit selection with a warning or require review? The captured descriptions already justify direct parent attribution for false; broader top-level or recursive inheritance remains unapproved.

After the user agrees to the taught design, begin with the typed cleaned-input reader and acceptance fixtures for the policy table, then implement pure decisions and coherent publication.
# Candidate 3: payments as an attribution graph

## Problem

Cleaning already gives stable IDs to payment occurrences and separate source-role observations. The resolver needs company identity links without making a company identity the payment, copying donor metadata across roles, or guessing which role represents “the funder.” This candidate makes those links explicit in a small graph artifact: occurrences point to role observations, observations may point to a source-reported company identity, and optional attribution views state which role a consumer is counting. Company numbers come only from captured donor evidence; there is no registry lookup or database write.

## Usage (caller's view)

The operator runs a separate offline stage against one cleaned run:

```text
data declarations resolve-funders CONFIG --ingestion-key UUID
```

The command reads the cleaned funding and observation tables and publishes a deterministic relationship artifact under `resolved/declarations/`. Consumers follow occurrence links and choose a role view explicitly:

```rust
let result = resolver.resolve(&cleaned)?;
let graph = result.relationships();
let attributed = graph.attributed_occurrences(AttributionView::UltimatePayer);
```

An analyst can inspect both declared donor and ultimate payer for the same payment:

```rust
for payment in graph.occurrences() { inspect(payment.id(), payment.roles()); }
```

Another caller counts a payment once while grouping by its selected role's resolved company:

```rust
for item in graph.attributed_occurrences(AttributionView::Donor) { totals.add(item.occurrence_id(), item.identity_key(), item.amount()); }
```

There is no default selected funder. Each view emits at most one row per `FundingEntryId`; declaration observations have no amount and never become synthetic payment rows.

## Shape

The graph has three node concepts and typed edges. A funding occurrence owns amount and payment facts. Its role edges reference source observations, preserving scope, pointer, role and name evidence. An observation can link to an identity only when its own captured company identifier parses as a Companies House number; current cleaner rules mean this is donor evidence. Equal parsed identifiers create one source-reported company identity even when observation names differ. This records the source's identifier, not independently verified Companies House membership.

Derived indexes map occurrence ID to occurrence, observation ID to observation, and company key to observations. Traversal and reverse inspection then use bounded lookup. Identity keys are deterministic and scoped as `companies-house/{digits}`. Parsing trims surrounding whitespace and accepts digits only; it preserves the digit sequence, including leading zeroes, and does not pad or claim registry validity. Missing or malformed IDs produce row outcomes without identity edges.

Names are not identity keys. Matching names never transfer donor metadata to payer or ultimate payer. Different company IDs remain distinct despite identical or similar names. Personal names, conjunctions, and aliases stay unsplit observations. Conflicting donor kinds and role flags remain evidence and produce conflict outcomes where applicable; no precedence rule is invented.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq)]
pub struct FundingEntryId(String);
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq)]
pub struct FunderObservationId(String);
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq, Ord, PartialOrd)]
pub struct CompanyNumber(String); // digits parsed from captured source value
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq, Ord, PartialOrd)]
pub struct CompanyIdentityKey(String); // "companies-house/{number}"

pub enum RoleLink {
    Observed { role: FunderRole, observation: FunderObservationId },
    Absent { role: FunderRole },
}
pub struct PaymentRelationship {
    occurrence: FundingEntryId,
    roles: [RoleLink; 3],
}
pub struct ObservationRelationship {
    observation: FunderObservationId,
    identity: Option<CompanyIdentityKey>,
    outcome: IdentityOutcome,
}
pub enum IdentityOutcome {
    LinkedByCompanyNumber,
    NoCompanyNumber,
    MalformedCompanyNumber,
    NameMissing,
    NameWithheld,
    NameOnlyUnresolved,
    ConflictingDonorEvidence,
}
pub enum AttributionView { Donor, Payer, UltimatePayer }
pub struct AttributedOccurrence<'a> {
    occurrence: &'a CleanedFundingEntry,
    role: FunderRole,
    observation: Option<&'a FunderObservation>,
    identity: Option<&'a CompanyIdentityKey>,
}
pub struct ResolutionRelationships { /* private nodes and derived indexes */ }
pub struct ResolutionResult { relationships: ResolutionRelationships, summary: ResolutionSummary }

impl ResolutionRelationships {
    pub fn occurrences(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &PaymentRelationship>;
    pub fn observation(&self, id: &FunderObservationId) -> Option<&ObservationRelationship>;
    pub fn company_observations(&self, id: &CompanyIdentityKey)
        -> impl Iterator<Item = &ObservationRelationship>;
    pub fn attributed_occurrences(&self, view: AttributionView)
        -> impl Iterator<Item = AttributedOccurrence<'_>>;
}

impl FunderResolver {
    pub fn resolve(&self, cleaned: &CleanedDeclarations)
        -> Result<ResolutionResult, EntityIngestionError>;
}
pub trait FunderResolutionStorage: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    async fn read_cleaned_declarations(&self) -> Result<CleanedDeclarations, EntityIngestionError>;
    async fn publish_funder_relationships(&self, result: &ResolutionResult)
        -> Result<(), EntityIngestionError>;
}
pub async fn resolve_funders<S: FunderResolutionStorage>(
    storage: &S,
) -> Result<ResolutionSummary, EntityIngestionError>;
```

The API uses domain values and hides Parquet fields and paths. `FunderResolver::resolve` is pure; storage owns decoding and publication. This small interface hides integrity checking, number parsing, deterministic graph construction and artifact writing, following type-first modeling and parse-at-boundary practice. No database repository is needed offline; later database access must use SQLx macros.

### Policy table

| Evidence / condition | Relationship decision | Row outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Donor has digits-only company number | Link donor observation to `companies-house/{digits}` | Linked by source-reported number |
| Payer/ultimate payer has same name as donor, no own company ID | Keep role edge; no identity edge | Name only unresolved |
| Two observations have equal company number and different names | Link both to same identity key | Linked by source-reported number |
| Different numbers, equal/similar names | Keep separate identity keys | Linked independently |
| Missing / malformed number | No identity edge; retain observation | No ID / malformed ID |
| Missing or withheld name with valid donor ID | Link by ID; preserve name status | Linked by number, name unavailable |
| `is_ultimate_payer_different=true` | Preserve flag; do not infer entity equality | Flag says different |
| Flag `false` or absent | Preserve value; do not infer same entity | Same-entity status unknown |
| Donor and ultimate payer both named; explicit withheld ultimate payer | Keep both observations and withheld status; no fallback | No selected role |
| Donor/payer names disagree or donor metadata conflicts | Keep role evidence; no cross-role metadata transfer | Conflict / unresolved |
| Parent declaration reference exists | Retain reference as context only; no parent payer substitution | No inherited attribution |
| Declaration observation alongside nested payments | Retain as declaration context, outside payment links | No amount-bearing row |

Legacy `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` suppresses parent fallback when `IsUltimatePayerDifferent` is true and permits it for false or absent. This does not establish that false means donor and ultimate payer are the same company. The proposal does not port parent fallback; field semantics remain open.

### Behavior and failure contract

Reject corrupt cleaned tables at input: duplicate IDs, dangling role references, mismatched occurrence links, or invalid required columns are dataset/read errors and publish nothing. Per-row uncertainty is data: missing/malformed IDs, absent/withheld or personal names, conjunctions, conflicting kinds and role-flag disagreement remain inspectable outcomes. Name-only evidence never creates identity; conflicts retain both observations.

Sort rows and keys by stable source IDs before serialization for repeatable logical output. Publish one coherent directory via temporary sibling staging and atomic rename; reject an existing destination and leave cleaned inputs intact. Interrupted staging is isolated, and retries use a fresh name, matching the current publication boundary.

Downstream counts use `FundingEntryId`, never graph edges. A view selects at most one role per occurrence; absent or unresolved roles remain countable in an unresolved bucket. Other role edges explain the payment and are not additional occurrences. Declaration-only observations cannot enter amount aggregation because they lack a funding edge.

### Acceptance cases

| Case | Expected result |
| --- | --- |
| Donor and ultimate payer have distinct names, only donor has company ID | Donor links by ID; ultimate payer remains unresolved; no default attribution |
| Same company ID on two donor observations with spelling variation | Shared deterministic company identity; both raw names retained |
| Same name but distinct donor IDs | Two company identities; names do not merge them |
| Ultimate payer explicitly withheld, donor named | Preserve both role states; ultimate-payer view is unresolved, with no donor fallback |
| Flag is true / false / absent | Preserve exact flag; only true blocks legacy parent fallback; none proves shared identity |
| Parent payer could historically fill missing child payer | No inherited role link in this proposal |
| Declaration-level donor coexists with nested payment donor | Declaration context retained; only nested occurrence carries amount |
| Malformed company number | Observation retained with malformed-ID outcome; other rows still resolve |

## Synthesis decision
Pending synthesis. This candidate deliberately explores explicit payment-to-role-to-identity relationships rather than making one observation mapping the whole product surface.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept graph structure and derived indexes for explicit provenance and safe role traversal.
- We accept multiple views and unresolved name-only rows to avoid silently choosing attribution or merging across roles.
- We accept unverified source-reported IDs and unresolved parent semantics to remain offline without inventing identity claims.

## Alternatives considered

- **Observation-to-identity mapping with attribution deferred (candidate 1):** compact and close to the existing table, but callers must assemble payment-role paths and avoid double counting; this graph makes occurrence ownership explicit at the cost of a richer output.
- **Typed payment-attribution decision beside identity mapping (candidate 2):** gives a direct answer but centralizes policy before the user chooses donor versus ultimate payer; this graph leaves role selection at the consumer boundary.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation has started. Keep proposed names and shapes aligned with the usage and outcome contracts if the design is approved; record any accepted deviation here before implementation proceeds.

## Open questions and risks

- Should the first command publish relationships only, or also named attribution-view totals for convenience?
- Should `is_ultimate_payer_different=true` mean only “do not inherit parent payer,” or is there another documented source meaning?
- Can the source ever place a company identifier on a non-donor observation, or does that remain outside the current capture contract?
- Should numeric company identifiers compare as exact captured digits, or is there authoritative evidence for normalizing leading zeroes?
- Is a source-reported equal company identifier sufficient to call the resulting key a company identity in product output, provided it is explicitly labeled unverified?

## Next implementation step

After the user discusses the attribution decision, implement the cleaned-table reader and pure relationship constructor against the accepted contract before wiring the CLI publisher.
# Design red flags

Screen every candidate before synthesis. A red flag is a reason to revise or reject the shape.

## Shallow module

A shallow module exposes a large interface while hiding little complexity. Judge depth by the capability and policy hidden behind the public surface relative to the size of that surface. Prefer a simple interface backed by substantial behavior.

Do not confuse a deep module with a deep call chain. A deep call chain scatters understanding across layers. A deep module concentrates capability behind one interface.

Look for these signs:

- Callers coordinate several methods to complete one operation.
- Public options expose internal stages or implementation choices.
- Learning the interface does not save the caller from learning the implementation.

## Information leakage

Information leakage makes multiple modules depend on the same internal decision. A representation, policy, or protocol detail appears in more than one place, so changing it requires coordinated edits.

Public re-exports of transport or wire types are leakage. Parse external data into domain types behind the interface. Keep storage schemas, framework objects, and protocol details private.

## Temporal decomposition

Temporal decomposition organizes modules by execution order instead of the knowledge they own. Separate load, validate, transform, and save stages often repeat one representation and its invariants across several boundaries.

Group code around domain knowledge and ownership. Methods that run at different times can still belong to one module when they protect the same decisions.

## Pass-through method

A pass-through method forwards the same arguments to another method with the same shape. It adds a layer without hiding complexity.

Remove it or move responsibility to the module that can complete the operation. Keep a forwarding boundary only when it adds policy, adaptation, or a distinct abstraction.

## Split ownership

More than one module writes the same state or keeps its own copy of it. An agent that edits one writer can't see the others, so their rules diverge.

Give each piece of state one owner. Other modules read it or ask the owner to change it.

## Two ways to do one task

The design supports more than one way to do the same task. An agent copies whichever way it finds first, so every way keeps gaining callers.

Keep one way. Move callers off the others and delete them in the same change.

## Importable internals

A caller can import a module's internals. An agent takes the shortest path that compiles, so it imports them directly and they become part of the interface.

Make internals unreachable from outside the module, so an import from outside fails the build.

## Hand-synced list

Two or more places list the same items, and adding an item means editing every list. An agent that sees one list updates only that one.

Keep one list and derive the others from it. If a list can't be derived, make the build fail when the lists disagree.
