The same capture has 2,467 raw rows with a non-null company number, all with source kind `Company`. Of these, 2,197 match eight digits or two letters followed by six digits after trimming. This pattern was an exploratory count, not a complete Companies House validator. Other values include `527227`, `0967733649`, `123`, and `NI 016363`.

Two primary documentation sources were read. No company-record API was called.

- [Companies House number-entry guidance](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company-lookup/search?forward=/auth-code-requests/company/%7BcompanyNumber%7D/confirm) specifies an eight-character number and instructs users to add leading zeros to shorter values. It gives a numeric example. A proposed numeric padding rule can cite this guidance; it is not proof that the source supplied the correct number.
- [Companies House URI guide](https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/426891/uniformResourceIdentifiersCustomerGuide.pdf), version 1.1, says prefixes use uppercase and lists legacy forms including `R0`. Its old prefix list is not a current exhaustive validator.

Design consequence: distinguish mechanical canonicalization from registry verification. Do not implement a guessed exhaustive prefix validator. Leave unusual or ambiguous numbers unresolved in the first version and retain their raw spelling. Deciding whether to pad short numeric values is an explicit policy choice supported by the entry guidance, not an API lookup.
# Resolve observations; select a role only when reporting

## Usage, caller's view

Proposal for discussion before implementation: keep donor, payer and ultimate-payer reporting as explicit, separate views. Do not publish a single selected funder. A declared-role view answers who was named in that role; an ultimate-source view also follows an explicitly supported parent-payer reference. A missing ultimate payer stays unknown unless that parent rule supplies evidence, even when the donor has a company number.

The proposed `declarations resolve --key <ingestion-key>` stage reads the existing cleaned pair and publishes an observation-to-company mapping offline. It neither fetches registry records nor rewrites funding amounts. Exact command spelling follows the existing CLI when implemented.

```rust
// CLI: the filesystem adapter already owns the explicit ingestion key.
let summary = DeclarationResolverService::new(storage)
    .resolve_declarations().await?;
println!("{} observations linked", summary.company_links());

// Consumer: explicitly ask the donor question, one row per occurrence.
let resolved = ResolvedDeclarations::new(cleaned, mapping)?;
for occurrence in resolved.funding_view(FundingView::DeclaredRole(FunderRole::Donor)) {
    display(occurrence.entry(), occurrence.observation(), occurrence.resolution());
}

// Consumer: ultimate-payer coverage includes absent and withheld observations.
let ultimate_rows = resolved.funding_view(FundingView::UltimateSource);
let coverage = count_available_names(ultimate_rows); // Not an identity count.
```

## Problem

The current `funder_id` names a source-role observation. Equal names do not establish identity, and donor company evidence does not belong to an ultimate payer. The legacy preferred-name rule mixes those questions. The next useful step is narrower: join observations carrying compatible source-reported company identifiers while leaving attribution visible to callers. This uses the cleaner's role references and stable occurrence IDs without changing the database's current unique-name model.

## Shape and module ownership

| Module | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `domain/models/declaration_resolution.rs` | Company-number parsing policy, observation outcomes, deterministic mapping, checked aggregate and explicit role views. Private collection fields prevent bypassing link invariants; parent-payer selection belongs to the derived view, not identity mapping. |
| `domain/repositories/declaration_resolution.rs` | Read a coherent typed cleaned dataset; publish a complete resolution artifact. No Arrow types escape. |
| `domain/services/declaration_resolution.rs` | One read/resolve/publish operation and summary; matches `DeclarationCleanerService`. |
| `outbound/declaration_resolution.rs` | Cleaned Parquet decoding, manifest digests, mapping encoding and atomic publication. Reuse filesystem paths and existing error conventions. |
| `inbound/cli.rs` and ingestion-stage enum | Add offline `resolve`; require key and loader configuration only. |

The service hides integrity checks, deterministic grouping and publication behind one call. The consumer selects its reporting question. Roles remain the existing `FunderRole` enum; there is no second list of role names. The pure model owns resolution, while the adapter parses wire fields and rejects structural corruption, per `boundary-discipline`.

