## Behavior contract and attribution policy

| Evidence | Proposed interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| Donor and ultimate payer both named | Preserve both. Each role view selects only its own observation, even if names match. The ultimate-source view uses the explicit ultimate observation. |
| Ultimate payer explicitly empty or withheld | Ultimate-payer view reports missing or withheld; never falls back to donor. |
| Ultimate-payer flag `true` | Preserve the claim of difference; absent ultimate-payer name remains unknown. No parent fallback. |
| Flag `false` | Ultimate-source view may use the same-member parent root payer; never equate local donor and ultimate payer. |
| Flag absent | Unknown flag, distinct from `false`; no implied agreement. |
| Donor and payer names disagree | Both remain usable role observations. Neither overrides the other. |
| Top-level names and nested occurrences | Declaration observations resolve independently; never propagate them to nested funding entries. |
| Parent reference | Resolve only a direct, unique, same-member root-level parent payer for the ultimate-source view and flag false. No amount or metadata copying. |

Captured descriptions now settle the comparison: `IsUltimatePayerDifferent` compares the child ultimate payer with the payer in the linked parent interest. It never compares the child's donor and ultimate payer. `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` also allowed absent flags, but this proposal permits inheritance only for explicit false. The captured case 5900 has no local names and flag false; ignoring parent attribution would leave all 1,498 financial rows of that shape unresolved, including this demonstrated parent-payer link. See `../source-evidence.md`.

`UltimateSource` first retains any explicit local ultimate-payer observation, including missing/withheld. Otherwise false plus a parent resolves a unique root-level payer observation indexed by member ID and parent declaration ID. Retain its observation ID, role and scope, and mark attribution `ParentPayer`; do not clone it as a child ultimate observation. An explicit withheld/empty local ultimate observation blocks inheritance. True blocks inheritance; absent stays unknown. Local donor or payer observations never substitute for the parent payer. If explicit false accompanies a local ultimate name inconsistent with the available parent name, emit `ConflictingEvidence` while both observations remain accessible in declared-role views. Equal names do not merge identities.

Missing parent evidence yields `MissingParentPayer`; multiple candidate parent payer observations yield `AmbiguousParentPayer`. Cross-member matches are ignored; self-parent references produce conflicting evidence. There is no recursive traversal, so a cycle cannot loop or supply a multi-hop fallback. A root payer may be declaration-scoped or funding-scoped when the parent has its own top-level amount. At decoding, classify its source pointer as exactly `/versions/<index>/fields`; never select `/values/` groups by ordinal or name. Only this direct parent root payer is supported; nested parent funding roles and multi-hop ancestry supply no fallback. Parent declaration-only observations need no amount or inherited flag. Child 5900 therefore refers to parent 5222's `Guardian News & Media Ltd` payer without creating a parent money row. Source replay would be needed before adding broader ancestry rules. The case 13091 uses explicit `Viking Penguin` with true; its parent 11317 payer stays distinct and its `via` text is never split.

Company parsing trims outer whitespace and uppercases ASCII letters; it preserves every digit, including leading zeros. Version 1 accepts exactly eight ASCII alphanumeric characters containing at least one digit as a conservative token envelope. This is an explicit supported-format policy, not a catalogue of valid Companies House prefixes or proof of registry membership. Other nonblank forms produce `UnsupportedCompanyNumber`; never pad, delete punctuation, infer a country, or call an API. Empty input is absent. Retain the original value through the observation reference.

The identity namespace is `source-reported:companies-house:<canonical-token>`. Its claim means that Parliament supplied this company identifier, not that the registry independently confirmed it. Missing donor kind or explicit `Company` permits that link. Explicit incompatible kinds produce `ConflictingDonorKind`; unknown kind text produces `UnsupportedDonorKind`. A conflicted observation stays unresolved even if another observation with the same number resolves. No last-write-wins entity kind exists.

Two eligible observations with the same identifier share a key despite different names. Different identifiers always remain different keys. Names, aliases, initials and token similarity do not merge any identities. Withheld names and missing names can still resolve from eligible identifier evidence. Personal-looking names or conjunctions are not split or classified from spelling; a compatible captured number still makes only a source-reported company claim. Otherwise each observation remains unresolved, with its original name status available to reports.

Current payer and ultimate-payer observations cannot receive a company link because cleaning attaches company numbers only to donors. Even an exact donor-name match does not change that. This limited coverage is honest and can expand later through a separately justified identity-evidence rule.

## Offline artifacts, consistency and counting

Publish `resolved/declarations/observation_resolution.parquet` plus a manifest. The table has exactly one row per observation: `observation_id`, `outcome`, nullable `company_identity_key`, nullable `unresolved_reason`. Outcome-specific column constraints come from the domain enum. The manifest records schema/resolver versions, input-file SHA-256 digests and row counts. No separate entity table or preferred-name record is necessary. For display, use the linked observation's original name, or the reported number if unnamed.

Sort mapping rows by observation ID. Identity keys are independent of ingestion key and row order; unresolved observations get no invented shared entity key. Observation IDs remain scoped to the manifest's exact dataset, since ordinal changes between captures can change their meaning. Equal keys across runs express equal source-reported identifiers, not a claim that observations are unchanged.

The adapter reads each cleaned file once into the checked dataset and binds its publication to those input digests. Reject missing columns, invalid enums, duplicate observation/occurrence IDs, wrong role/scope references, inconsistent ordinals and dangling funding references before writing. Malformed company strings, withheld names and conflicting donor kinds are row outcomes and do not fail the run. Missing files and read/write failures use storage errors; structural input failures use the existing data/read-error conventions with path context.

Write both artifacts into a unique sibling staging directory, close and sync them, then publish with an atomic no-replace directory operation. A completed existing destination is an explicit error; a repeat never silently overwrites. Concurrent attempts own different staging directories and only one can publish, per `separate-before-serializing-shared-state`. A crash leaves either a complete result or an ignored staging directory. A future policy version requires a new run/output location, not editing an existing artifact.

Each view iterates funding entries first and selects at most one observation, locally or through the direct parent index. It emits one row for every occurrence, including absent roles, so an amount appears once per view. A report must choose one versioned view policy for financial totals; unioning roles is a relationship report, not an additive money table. To ask whether a company occurs in any role, select distinct funding-entry IDs before joining amounts. Declaration-scoped observations never create funding rows. Currency/amount parsing remains outside this identity stage.

## Acceptance cases

| Input | Required result |
| --- | --- |
| `Acme Ltd / 01234567` and `Acme Holdings / 01234567` on eligible donors | One company key, two observations; all occurrences retained. |
| Same donor name with `01234567` and `07654321` | Two company keys; no name bridge. |
| Donor has number; identical ultimate-payer name | Donor resolves; ultimate payer remains unresolved. |
| Donor named; ultimate payer withheld; flag true | Ultimate view keeps withheld observation, with no fallback. |
| Flag false; no local ultimate name; unique same-member parent payer | Ultimate-source view references parent payer with provenance; donor remains separate. |
| Flag absent, or true with unnamed ultimate payer | No parent fallback; ultimate source remains unknown. |
| Flag false with local ultimate name disagreeing with parent payer | Conflicting evidence in ultimate-source view; declared-role views preserve names. |
| `1234567`, `SC-12345`, whitespace-only identifier | Unsupported, unsupported, absent respectively; no fabricated digits. |
| ` sc123456 ` and `SC123456`, eligible kinds | Same source-reported key; no registry-verification claim. |
| Number with `Individual`; same number elsewhere with `Company` | Individual observation unresolved; compatible company observation resolves. |
| Missing/withheld name with eligible number | Company link with name status unchanged. |
| `Jane Doe`, `A and B`, no number | Separate unresolved observations; no person merge or splitting. |
| Top-level payer, two nested entries lacking payer, no supported parent rule | Declaration observation retained; two missing payer-view rows, no local propagation. |
| Parent missing, wrong member, multiple parent payer observations, self-reference | Missing or ambiguous/conflicting attribution outcome; never a guessed payer. |
| Duplicate identical payments | Distinct occurrence IDs and two view rows; no deduplication by name/value. |
| Malformed reference, I/O failure, existing final destination | Fail with no new completed output. |

## Tradeoffs, alternatives and review

We accept unresolved payer identities and explicit report-role selection in exchange for a small, auditable identity rule. We accept conservative unsupported-number outcomes rather than guessing corrections. The most consequential product decision remains this proposal to offer separate role views without a preferred funder.

A typed payment-attribution decision model would hide fallback and withholding rules behind a single answer per occurrence. It is better once the user chooses a preferred funding question, but today it would encode unsettled role semantics into every result. A general relationship graph would express arbitrary ancestry and controller relationships, but callers would need traversal and cardinality rules for ordinary totals. The mapping plus fixed role views hides those joins without introducing that graph language.

Red-flag screening: no public Arrow schemas; no duplicated role registry; one pure owner of company eligibility and identity keys; one publication writer; no intermediate persistence stages; no generic matching engine or pass-through search service. The service's storage boundary is justified by offline I/O and atomic publication, not merely forwarding a model call. A consumer cannot construct an unchecked aggregate or modify its private indexes. Parent attribution is derived from checked evidence in this module rather than copied into the identity mapping.

Synthesis decision: reserved for the parent architect. Implementation reconciliation: none; this package contains no implementation.

Open question for the checkpoint: does the user want these explicit role views, or a single preferred-funder report with a separately approved fallback policy? Is the conservative eight-character envelope sufficient initially, retaining known short numeric values, `NI 016363` and ten-digit strings as unsupported? Source entry guidance supports numeric padding, but this candidate deliberately defers that canonicalization choice.

After the user has been taught and accepted the design, first implement the checked cleaned-input reader and pure mapping against the acceptance cases. No database schema change or website search dependency is needed; any later database adapter must use SQLx macros.
# Candidate 2: payment decisions with separate identity evidence

## Usage, caller's view

Proposal for discussion before implementation: each funding occurrence receives one typed reporting decision. Prefer an explicitly named ultimate payer; use the linked parent payer when the child explicitly reports the same ultimate payer; otherwise use donor or payer as a clearly labelled fallback only when evidence permits. A fallback means "reported donor" or "reported payer", never "confirmed ultimate funder".

The proposed offline command is `exposed declarations resolve --ingestion-key <key>`. It reads the existing cleaned pair and publishes a new resolved directory. It requires neither `DATABASE_URL` nor an API client. The actual command spelling should follow the existing CLI stage parser.

```rust
// CLI owns configuration and invokes one service operation.
let summary = DeclarationResolutionService::new(storage)
    .resolve(key, AttributionPolicy::UltimateAwareV1).await?;

// Report consumer handles a decision without guessing its role or confidence.
match occurrence.attribution() {
    AttributionDecision::Selected(selection) => report(selection.subject(), selection.basis()),
    AttributionDecision::Unattributed(reason) => report_unknown(reason),
}

// Identity browsing retains observations even when no payment is attributable.
for observation in resolved.observations_for(&company_key) {
    display_source_role(observation.role(), observation.source_pointer());
}
```

## Problem and module map

Cleaning preserves donor, payer and ultimate-payer observations; only donor observations carry company numbers. `FundingEntryId` identifies money occurrences, while `FunderObservationId` identifies evidence. Choosing a reporting role cannot resolve identity, and resolving a company cannot prove that it ultimately paid.

| Owner | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `domain/models/declaration_resolution.rs` | Private constructors, identity evidence, attribution outcomes, pure whole-bundle resolution |
| `domain/services/declaration_resolution.rs` | Load typed cleaned input, resolve, publish, return summary |
| `domain/ports/declaration_resolution_storage.rs` | Read coherent inputs and publish one coherent result |
| `outbound/declaration_resolution.rs` | Arrow/Parquet parsing, referential checks, manifest and filesystem publication |
| Existing ingestion CLI | Add resolve stage and explicit key, construct only filesystem dependencies |

One model owns the decision policy. Callers do not chain role selection, metadata copying and matching helpers. The service hides those operations behind one command while exposing their recorded results. Wire columns remain inside the adapter. No DB schema or website search changes are required; a later database adapter must use SQLx macros.

## Shape

```rust
// Proposed sketches only. Fields are private; checked bundle construction is adapter-owned.
