## Behavior contract and attribution policy

| Evidence | Proposed interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| Donor and ultimate payer both named | Preserve both. Each role view selects only its own observation, even if names match. The ultimate-source view uses the explicit ultimate observation. |
| Ultimate payer explicitly empty or withheld | Ultimate-payer view reports missing or withheld; never falls back to donor. |
| Ultimate-payer flag `true` | Preserve the claim of difference; absent ultimate-payer name remains unknown. No parent fallback. |
| Flag `false` | Ultimate-source view may use the same-member parent root payer; never equate local donor and ultimate payer. |
| Flag absent | Unknown flag, distinct from `false`; no implied agreement. |
| Donor and payer names disagree | Both remain usable role observations. Neither overrides the other. |
| Top-level names and nested occurrences | Declaration observations resolve independently; never propagate them to nested funding entries. |
| Parent reference | Resolve only a direct, unique, same-member root-level parent payer for the ultimate-source view and flag false. No amount or metadata copying. |

Captured descriptions now settle the comparison: `IsUltimatePayerDifferent` compares the child ultimate payer with the payer in the linked parent interest. It never compares the child's donor and ultimate payer. `FieldGroup::parent_payer_applies` also allowed absent flags, but this proposal permits inheritance only for explicit false. The captured case 5900 has no local names and flag false; ignoring parent attribution would leave all 1,498 financial rows of that shape unresolved, including this demonstrated parent-payer link. See `../source-evidence.md`.

`UltimateSource` first retains any explicit local ultimate-payer observation, including missing/withheld. Otherwise false plus a parent resolves a unique root-level payer observation indexed by member ID and parent declaration ID. Retain its observation ID, role and scope, and mark attribution `ParentPayer`; do not clone it as a child ultimate observation. An explicit withheld/empty local ultimate observation blocks inheritance. True blocks inheritance; absent stays unknown. Local donor or payer observations never substitute for the parent payer. If explicit false accompanies a local ultimate name inconsistent with the available parent name, emit `ConflictingEvidence` while both observations remain accessible in declared-role views. Equal names do not merge identities.

Missing parent evidence yields `MissingParentPayer`; multiple candidate parent payer observations yield `AmbiguousParentPayer`. Cross-member matches are ignored; self-parent references produce conflicting evidence. There is no recursive traversal, so a cycle cannot loop or supply a multi-hop fallback. A root payer may be declaration-scoped or funding-scoped when the parent has its own top-level amount. At decoding, classify its source pointer as exactly `/versions/<index>/fields`; never select `/values/` groups by ordinal or name. Only this direct parent root payer is supported; nested parent funding roles and multi-hop ancestry supply no fallback. Parent declaration-only observations need n…5758 tokens truncated…back |
| Donor A, absent UP, false versus absent | False selects eligible parent payer or remains unknown; absent permits donor fallback; no donor/UP identity implication |
| Donor A and payer B, absent UP/flag | Donor fallback; payer B retained separately |
| Two donors, names A/B, same accepted number | One reported company key, two source observations and occurrences |
| Equal names, distinct accepted company IDs | Distinct company keys; no fuzzy merge |
| Donor name withheld, accepted ID, absent UP/flag | Donor fallback with company identity and withheld display name |
| Short number, person name, conjunction or non-company kind | Preserve raw evidence and relevant issue; no name-based merge or split |
| Same-member parent payer plus unnamed child payment, false | Select parent payer once; keep child money row and parent observation |
| Parent payer plus child UP Viking Penguin, true | Select child UP; preserve parent `via` name unsplit |
| Parent payer plus child flag absent or true without UP | Absent does not inherit; true remains unknown |
| Missing/withheld/cyclic parent with false | Row-level unattributed parent reason; no donor substitution |
| Child top-level payer plus unnamed nested payment | Context unresolved; no arbitrary top-level inheritance |
| Declaration-only donor with accepted ID | Identity row, zero funding or attribution rows |
| Second identical run or crash during staging | Verified existing bundle succeeds; no replacement or partial published bundle |

## Rationale and alternatives

We accept conservative unknown identity for payer/UP in exchange for company resolution supported by captured evidence. We accept a donor fallback that does not prove ultimate funding in exchange for a useful one-row reporting result whose basis is explicit. Consumers must label fallback totals accordingly.

An identity-only mapping with explicit role views has a smaller command contract and leaves attribution fully open. It loses here because each reporting consumer must repeat withholding, flag and fallback rules. A relationship graph hides traversal and evidence storage well, but callers must still choose payment attribution and avoid multiplying money through role edges; it adds a general traversal interface beyond this command's needs. This typed decision model centralizes that reporting policy while retaining observations for later reinterpretation.

Red-flag screening: one public service operation; private Arrow types; one policy owner; one publication owner; no exposed internal stage sequence; no no-op legacy persistence path; identity derived from observation evidence and selected identity derived by lookup rather than synchronized copies. The in-memory reverse index is derived, never published as another writable truth.

Synthesis decision belongs to the parent review; this candidate does not select itself. Implementation reconciliation is empty because no implementation is authorized at this checkpoint.

Open questions for discussion: should primary reporting prefer explicit ultimate payer with labelled donor fallback, or should donor and ultimate-payer reports remain separately selected views? Is the narrow company-number grammar appropriate for the captured data? Should conflicting explicit UP and false parent evidence retain explicit selection with a warning or require review? The captured descriptions already justify direct parent attribution for false; broader top-level or recursive inheritance remains unapproved.

After the user agrees to the taught design, begin with the typed cleaned-input reader and acceptance fixtures for the policy table, then implement pure decisions and coherent publication.
# Candidate 3: payments as an attribution graph

## Problem

Cleaning already gives stable IDs to payment occurrences and separate source-role observations. The resolver needs company identity links without making a company identity the payment, copying donor metadata across roles, or guessing which role represents “the funder.” This candidate makes those links explicit in a small graph artifact: occurrences point to role observations, observations may point to a source-reported company identity, and optional attribution views state which role a consumer is counting. Company numbers come only from captured donor evidence; there is no registry lookup or database write.

## Usage (caller's view)

The operator runs a separate offline stage against one cleaned run:

```text
data declarations resolve-funders CONFIG --ingestion-key UUID
```

The command reads the cleaned funding and observation tables and publishes a deterministic relationship artifact under `resolved/declarations/`. Consumers follow occurrence links and choose a role view explicitly:

```rust
let result = resolver.resolve(&cleaned)?;
let graph = result.relationships();
let attributed = graph.attributed_occurrences(AttributionView::UltimatePayer);
```

An analyst can inspect both declared donor and ultimate payer for the same payment:

```rust
for payment in graph.occurrences() { inspect(payment.id(), payment.roles()); }
```

Another caller counts a payment once while grouping by its selected role's resolved company:

```rust
for item in graph.attributed_occurrences(AttributionView::Donor) { totals.add(item.occurrence_id(), item.identity_key(), item.amount()); }
```

There is no default selected funder. Each view emits at most one row per `FundingEntryId`; declaration observations have no amount and never become synthetic payment rows.

## Shape

The graph has three node concepts and typed edges. A funding occurrence owns amount and payment facts. Its role edges reference source observations, preserving scope, pointer, role and name evidence. An observation can link to an identity only when its own captured company identifier parses as a Companies House number; current cleaner rules mean this is donor evidence. Equal parsed identifiers create one source-reported company identity even when observation names differ. This records the source's identifier, not independently verified Companies House membership.

Derived indexes map occurrence ID to occurrence, observation ID to observation, and company key to observations. Traversal and reverse inspection then use bounded lookup. Identity keys are deterministic and scoped as `companies-house/{digits}`. Parsing trims surrounding whitespace and accepts digits only; it preserves the digit sequence, including leading zeroes, and does not pad or claim registry validity. Missing or malformed IDs produce row outcomes without identity edges.

Names are not identity keys. Matching names never transfer donor metadata to payer or ultimate payer. Different company IDs remain distinct despite identical or similar names. Personal names, conjunctions, and aliases stay unsplit observations. Conflicting donor kinds and role flags remain evidence and produce conflict outcomes where applicable; no precedence rule is invented.

